{"name":"Ibis White","hex":"#f2ece6","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"ibis","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any of various long-legged wading birds in the family Threskiornithidae, having long downcurved bills used to probe the mud for prey such as crustaceans."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/ibis"},{"term":"white","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Bright and colourless; reflecting equal quantities of all frequencies of visible light.","Of or relating to Europeans or those of European descent, regardless if their skin has cool or warm undertones."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The color of snow or milk; the color of light containing equal amounts of all visible wavelengths.","A person of European descent with light-colored skin."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/white"}],"entries":[{"title":"Ibis","blocks":[{"text":"The ibis are a group of long-legged wading birds in the family Threskiornithidae that inhabit wetlands, forests and plains. \"Ibis\" derives from the Latin and Ancient Greek word for this group of birds. It also occurs in the scientific name of the western cattle egret mistakenly identified in 1757 as being the sacred ibis."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c1/Black-headed_Ibis_%28Threskiornis_melanocephalus%29.jpg/330px-Black-headed_Ibis_%28Threskiornis_melanocephalus%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ibis","wikidata":"Q193833"},{"title":"White","blocks":[{"text":"White is the lightest color and is achromatic. It is the color of objects such as snow, chalk, and milk, and is the opposite of black. White objects fully reflect and scatter all the visible wavelengths of light. White on television and computer screens is created by a mixture of red, blue, and green light. The color white can be given with white pigments, especially titanium dioxide."}],"ima